Below is a short, self‑contained C++ program that demonstrates the most common ways to use references:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

// ---------- 1. Simple lvalue reference ----------
void addTen(int &n)          // n is an lvalue reference to an int
{
    n += 10;                 // changes the original object
}

// ---------- 2. Returning a reference ----------
int& getLargest(int &a, int &b)   // returns a reference to the larger of a, b
{
    return (a > b) ? a : b;
}

// ---------- 3. Const reference ----------
void printLength(const std::string &s)   // s can be bound to a temporary
{
    std::cout << "Length: " << s.length() << '\n';
}

// ---------- 4. Rvalue reference ----------
void moveString(std::string &&s)   // s is an rvalue reference
{
    std::string local = std::move(s); // transfer ownership
    std::cout << "Moved string: " << local << '\n';
}

int main()
{
    // 1. Simple aliasing
    int a = 5;
    int &refA = a;           // refA is an alias of a
    std::cout << "a = " << a << ", refA = " << refA << '\n';

    refA = 20;                // changing refA changes a
    std::cout << "After refA = 20, a = " << a << '\n';

    // 2. Function that modifies via reference
    addTen(a);                // a becomes 30
    std::cout << "After addTen, a = " << a << '\n';

    // 3. Returning a reference
    int b = 15;
    int &maxRef = getLargest(a, b);   // maxRef refers to a (since 30 > 15)
    std::cout << "Largest: " << maxRef << '\n';

    maxRef = 25;              // changes a via the returned reference
    std::cout << "After maxRef = 25, a = " << a << '\n';

    // 4. Const reference can bind to temporaries
    printLength("Hello, world!");     // temporary std::string is created

    // 5. Rvalue reference (moves a temporary)
    moveString(std::string("Move me")); // std::string temporary bound to rvalue ref

    return 0;
}
```

### What the program shows

| Feature | Code | Explanation |
|---------|------|-------------|
| **Lvalue reference (`int &`)** | `int &refA = a;` | `refA` is just another name for `a`. Any change to `refA` changes `a`. |
| **Reference as a function parameter** | `void addTen(int &n)` | The function receives an alias to the caller’s variable, allowing direct modification. |
| **Reference return value** | `int &getLargest(int &a, int &b)` | Returns a reference to the larger argument, so the caller can modify that argument via the returned reference. |
| **Const reference** | `void printLength(const std::string &s)` | Can bind to rvalues (temporaries) and prevents modification of the underlying object. |
| **Rvalue reference (`T &&`)** | `void moveString(std::string &&s)` | Binds to temporaries, enabling move semantics (efficient resource transfer). |

Compile this with any C++11‑compatible compiler (`g++ -std=c++11 -Wall example.cpp`) and run it to observe the output and the effects of references.
